      "writtenOn": "2024-08-26T20:23:31Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The `unsafe` keyword has different meaning depending on the context.  Typically `unsafe` can appear in 2 contexts:\n\n* `unsafe fn` and/or `unsafe trait` should have a doc comment with safety requirements section.  For example - see https://rust-lang.github.io/rust-clippy/master/index.html#/missing_safety_doc\n* `unsafe { some-expr-here }` should have a comment explaining why the safety requirements are met (and why safe alternatives can\u0027t be used instead).  For example - see https://rust-lang.github.io/rust-clippy/master/index.html#/undocumented_unsafe_blocks\n\nHere (on new line 69 above), `unsafe` is used in yet another (more rare) context - this `unsafe` is consumed by the `cxx::bridge` proc macro:\n\n* This means that there is no `unsafe fn` available to the rest of Rust code here (and so the `missing_safety_doc` lint mentioned above doesn\u0027t apply here).  (There may be some private `unsafe fn` and `unsafe { expr }` within `cxx` implementation and/or generated code.)\n* Motivation for this `unsafe`:\n    * FFI is inherently unsafe.  `cxx` takes care of most of the unsafety (e.g. enforces at compile time that types/functions exist with the right signature on both sides of the FFI boundary), but some unsafety remains (e.g. when calling `fn is_srgb(self: \u0026Reader)` above, the C++ caller has to pinky-promise that they don\u0027t pass a dangling pointer).  Here the `unsafe` keyword is supposed to be an extra warning that the C++ caller has to be really, really careful (because the lifetimes are more complicated than in the `is_srgb` example - in `try_get_iccp` the lifetime of `iccp` has to be shorter than the lifetime of `self`)\n    \nI hope this helps?  Let me resolve this code review thread, but feel free to reactivate if needed.",
